{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on checker item 3 (9873fe58): choice (a). The fork's setup credential becomes a hold@2 token record with purpose setup, and the existing revoke rule owns its revocation\n\nDecision. Land no known credential that nothing owes. The repair uses forms the authority note already has (section 5.7, the token record: minting, live, revoking, ended; purposes workspace, fork read, staging, check read) and adds one purpose, setup. No new record kind, no private outbox, no second operation owner outside the journal.\n\nThe rule, stated for the head.\n1. When a fork attempt opens (the head rule's confirmed answer opens fork; a refused fork answer opens the next attempt), the same effect makes a token record minting with purpose setup, keyed by the capability's counter, whose nonsecret handle is the attempt name workspace:\u003cattempt-name\u003e. The plaintext stays in the host's private custody under that handle, as now.\n2. On a confirmed fork answer, selected or not, the fork rule moves that token to live and opens its revoke operation in the same outcome, exactly as repository creation returns its held handle for a later revoke (artifacts-host.ts:275). A refused fork answer moves the token to ended with no revoke, because the provider issued nothing; the host must not keep plaintext for a refused attempt, and the test shows the custody row absent.\n3. An unknown fork answer leaves the token minting, as section 5.7 says of any mint whose outcome is unknown. The late confirmed unselected answer that today opens a deletion (hold.ts:970) also moves the token to live and opens its revoke. So every credential the provider could have issued is owed a revocation by a judged operation, and the unknown case never loses the handle.\n4. The revoke operation's confirmed outcome ends the token; an unknown revoke keeps it revoking under the existing revoke rule's behavior (section 5.4 rule 4, form O12). Nothing in the host retries on its own.\n5. The hold's end and the fork's deletion do not wait for a setup revocation; they run as now, and an unrevoked setup token stays revoking until its revoke confirms. The host's existing revokeCredential takes the workspace: handle; no new host primitive.\n\nConditions.\n- The kernel-gate section names the setup purpose and the fork rule's two added effects, with the recomputed closure numbers for head and fork, alongside the additions item 4 asks for.\n- A scope test shows: fork created and the revoke times out (token revoking, plaintext held, revoke operation open); a later confirmed revoke ends it and the plaintext is deleted; a refused attempt leaves no token and no custody row; an unknown attempt then a late confirmed answer opens both the deletion and the revoke. Control: with the setup effect removed, the orphan credential test fails because nothing owes the revoke.\n- docs/hosts.md states that every provider credential the fork adapter mints is a token record owed a revocation; no open limit on this point.\n- This repair goes in the same recut as the other four items, then one full gate at the final head and a review request with --replace. The live witness after landing includes one deliberate revoke timeout only if the provider offers a way to force it; otherwise the scope test is the witness and the note says so.\nRetire this question when read.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9873fe588167614971ab319c80fb52a3743c8087 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0a0b8626cdd726c317fafe42974a68c1cfa2da4b, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:da1ba9e4dfc2ea012511e39a9c3ec5e3be8d2af2","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}